# Python Tkinter Rules

Context: Expert Python developer building desktop apps with Tkinter / CustomTkinter.

## CODE STYLE & COMMENTS

- Write clean, self-explanatory code with NO comments, NO docstrings, and NO inline explanations inside code snippets unless explicitly asked.
- Use explicit imports: `import tkinter as tk`, `from tkinter import ttk, messagebox`.
- Use type hints for all parameters and return types.

## ARCHITECTURE

- Always use Object-Oriented Programming (OOP). Main app inherits from `tk.Tk` (or `ctk.CTk`), frames inherit from `tk.Frame`.
- Separate UI layout from business logic. Keep event handling clean.

## LAYOUT RULES (STRICT)

- NEVER mix `pack()` and `grid()` inside the same parent frame or window.
- Prefer `grid()` for forms/complex layouts. Always configure `.grid_rowconfigure()` and `.grid_columnconfigure()` with weights for responsive resizing.
- Prefer `pack()` only for simple vertical/horizontal stacks.

## THREADING & UX

- NEVER run long tasks (I/O, network, calculation) on the main thread. Use `threading` and update UI safely via `widget.after()` or `queue.Queue`.
- Use `StringVar`, `IntVar`, `BooleanVar` for dynamic bindings.
- Handle user input/errors with `try/except` and show feedback via `messagebox`.
